{"name":"University of California Gold","hex":"#b78727","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"university","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An institution of higher education that provides facilities for teaching, research, and the conferral of academic degrees across undergraduate, graduate, and often professional levels.","The entirety of a group; all members of a class."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/university"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"California Gold","blocks":[{"text":"California Gold was an American soccer team which began playing under the Gold moniker in 2002. The club was founded in 1997 as the Stanislaus County Cruisers. The team was a member of the United Soccer Leagues Premier Development League (PDL), the fourth tier of the American Soccer Pyramid, until 2006, when the team left the league and the franchise was terminated."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/72/California_Gold_%28shield%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_Gold","wikidata":"Q5020506"},{"title":"Gold in California","blocks":[{"text":"Gold became highly concentrated in California, United States as the result of global forces operating over hundreds of millions of years. Volcanoes, tectonic plates and erosion all combined to concentrate billions of dollars' worth of gold in the mountains of California. During the California Gold Rush, gold-seekers known as \"Forty-Niners\" retrieved this gold, at first using simple techniques, and then developing more sophisticated techniques, which spread around the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Gold-210796.jpg/330px-Gold-210796.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_in_California","wikidata":"Q5578930"},{"title":"University of California","blocks":[{"text":"The University of California (UC) is California's system of public research universities. Headquartered in Oakland, the system is composed of ten campuses at Berkeley, Davis, Irvine, Los Angeles, Merced, Riverside, San Diego, San Francisco, Santa Barbara, and Santa Cruz. The system is the state's land-grant university. Under the California Master Plan for Higher Education, the University of California is a part of the state's three-system public postsecondary education plan, which also includes the California Community Colleges and California State University systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Seal_of_the_University_of_California.svg/330px-Seal_of_the_University_of_California.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California","wikidata":"Q184478"},{"title":"University","blocks":[{"text":"A university is an institution of tertiary education and research which awards academic degrees in several academic disciplines. University is derived from the Latin phrase universitas magistrorum et scholarium, which roughly means \"community of teachers and scholars\". Universities typically offer both undergraduate and postgraduate programs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Columbia_University%2C_NYC_%28June_2014%29_-_09.JPG/330px-Columbia_University%2C_NYC_%28June_2014%29_-_09.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University","wikidata":"Q3918"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/72/California_Gold_%28shield%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/72/California_Gold_%28shield%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalgold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Subduction_magma_rising.jpg/250px-Subduction_magma_rising.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Subduction_magma_rising.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Subduction_magma_rising.jpg/250px-Subduction_magma_rising.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gold-bearing magma rising after being subducted under the continental crust.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASubduction_magma_rising.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Bay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg/500px-Bay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Bay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg/1280px-Bay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Bay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg/500px-Bay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In November 1857, the College of California's trustees began to acquire various parcels of land facing the Golden Gate in what is now Berkeley.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABay_Blue_-_Flickr_-_Joe_Parks.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/South_Hall_UC_Berkeley.jpg/500px-South_Hall_U